From 24747504ef8875a147a442ebbf87ae573d2d364b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 10:37:42 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 14/15] server: answer a batch of clients together

`handle_events` walked the batch one client after another, so the batch cost the sum
of its clients. On a pruned node a single request can reach for a block the node has
discarded, seconds rather than milliseconds, and one wallet with deep history made
every other client in the batch wait for it in turn. Answered together, a batch costs
its slowest client instead.

Split the batch in three. Connects and disconnects mutate the peer map, so they stay
serial, and the clients with work are taken out of the map so each task owns one
outright. Answering runs across them in parallel, each task touching only its own
`Peer` and a shared `&Rpc` -- the same split `notify_peers` has always relied on.
Survivors go back in the map serially.

This does *not* take the work off the server loop, and the comments say so. `serve()`
still calls `handle_events` inline between calls to `rpc.sync()` and does not drain
`server_rx` again until it returns, so a slow batch still stalls indexing and a
request arriving mid-batch still waits for that batch to end. Only the cost within a
batch is addressed here. Moving this onto its own thread is the remaining piece and
is deliberately not attempted.

Behaviour per client is unchanged: its requests are answered in one batch, in the
order they arrived, a write error still disconnects it, and a disconnect still
discards whatever was queued behind it. The folding that carries those two ordering
rules is now `fold_messages`, which needs no live `Rpc` and so has tests.

Also corrects the write-timeout rationale in `accept_loop`. A wedged peer now holds a
worker rather than the loop directly, but the loop waits for that worker, so it still
holds up everything behind it -- the timeout matters for the same reason as before.
